\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {10}{\ignorespaces Clean-input $R^2$ per held-out test day. All seeds are plotted at the same day position; colours and marker shapes distinguish them. The solid line joins the mean of the five seeds, and the black diamonds are the five-seed ensemble of \eqref  {eq:ensemble}. The gap between the solid line and the diamonds is the gain from averaging predictions rather than scores. $R^2$ is the average of the $v_x$ and $v_y$ scores; $0$ is the constant-mean predictor.}}{19}{figure.caption.34}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:ensemble}{{10}{19}{Clean-input $R^2$ per held-out test day. All seeds are plotted at the same day position; colours and marker shapes distinguish them. The solid line joins the mean of the five seeds, and the black diamonds are the five-seed ensemble of \eqref {eq:ensemble}. The gap between the solid line and the diamonds is the gain from averaging predictions rather than scores. $R^2$ is the average of the $v_x$ and $v_y$ scores; $0$ is the constant-mean predictor}{figure.caption.34}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {11}{\ignorespaces From $R^2$ to centimetres per second, all on clean input. (a) measured and predicted $v_x$ over the first 10~s of one held-out session. (b) predicted against measured velocity for every scored bin of that session, with the diagonal as a perfect forecast; the prediction is compressed toward zero, which is why $R^2$ stays positive while the scatter is wide. (c) per-day Euclidean velocity RMSE, $\sqrt  {e_{v_x}^2+e_{v_y}^2}$, against the constant-mean predictor, whose error is $\sqrt  {2}\,\mathrm  {SD}(\text  {speed})$. Day identity is on the axis because RMSE is not comparable across days of different speed.}}{19}{figure.caption.36}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:r2rmse}{{11}{19}{From $R^2$ to centimetres per second, all on clean input. (a) measured and predicted $v_x$ over the first 10~s of one held-out session. (b) predicted against measured velocity for every scored bin of that session, with the diagonal as a perfect forecast; the prediction is compressed toward zero, which is why $R^2$ stays positive while the scatter is wide. (c) per-day Euclidean velocity RMSE, $\sqrt {e_{v_x}^2+e_{v_y}^2}$, against the constant-mean predictor, whose error is $\sqrt {2}\,\mathrm {SD}(\text {speed})$. Day identity is on the axis because RMSE is not comparable across days of different speed}{figure.caption.36}{}}
